---
import LegalDocument from "@/components/legal-document.astro";
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/base-layout.astro";

const title = "Privacy Policy — MemeOver";
const description =
	"How MemeOver processes Discord events, local app data, operational logs, and deletion requests.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<LegalDocument
		kicker="Privacy"
		title="Privacy Policy"
		description="MemeOver is a personal, open-source project. It is designed as a pass-through between one watched Discord channel and connected desktop overlays, not as a media hosting or analytics platform."
		updated="Last updated: May 25, 2026"
		langSwitchHref="/fr/confidentialite"
		langSwitchLabel="Version française"
	>
		<section>
			<h2>Who Runs MemeOver</h2>
			<p>
				MemeOver is published by Simon Hazard, an individual based in France. The source code is
				available on <a href="https://github.com/SimonHazard/MemeOver">GitHub</a> under the MIT
				license. The public website is hosted on Cloudflare Workers. The hosted Discord bot is
				self-hosted by the publisher in France. Operational logs are sent to Better Stack.
			</p>
			<p>
				For privacy or deletion requests, use the contact links on
				<a href="https://www.simonhazard.com/">simonhazard.com</a> or open a private-sensitive
				request from the <a href="https://github.com/SimonHazard/MemeOver/issues">GitHub project</a>
				without posting secrets or private media URLs.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>What MemeOver Does</h2>
			<p>
				MemeOver listens to a Discord server that has explicitly configured the bot. When a
				message, media attachment, sticker, reaction, or short text is posted in a watched
				channel, the bot sends a realtime event to desktop apps connected with that server's
				MemeOver credentials.
			</p>
			<p>
				MemeOver does <strong>not</strong> upload or re-host the media files. Media remains hosted
				by Discord or by the linked provider, such as Tenor, Giphy, or Imgur. Connected desktop
				apps load those URLs directly to display the overlay.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Data Processed By The Bot</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Server configuration:</strong> Discord server ID, watched channel IDs, and a
					random MemeOver connection token. This is stored on the bot host until the server is
					removed or deletion is requested.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Realtime Discord events:</strong> server ID, channel ID, message ID, author ID,
					username, optional server display name, avatar URL, media URL, media type, short text
					caption, reaction metadata, and timestamp. These events are transmitted to authenticated
					desktop clients connected to the same MemeOver server token.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Operational logs:</strong> timestamp, severity, module, event name, media type,
					media host, hashed Discord references, hashed media path, and technical errors. Logging
					is configured not to retain full media URLs, message text, or connection tokens.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The bot only processes Discord API data for the functionality described here. Discord
				requires developers to publish and follow a privacy policy for API Data; see the
				<a href="https://support-dev.discord.com/hc/en-us/articles/8562894815383-Discord-Developer-Terms-of-Service">
					Discord Developer Terms
				</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Purposes And Legal Bases</h2>
			<p>
				MemeOver processes personal data only for the purposes needed to run the service requested
				by Discord servers that configure the bot:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Service delivery:</strong> storing server configuration, authenticating desktop
					apps, and forwarding Discord events to connected overlays. The legal basis is the
					legitimate interest in providing the service explicitly enabled by server managers.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Security and reliability:</strong> limiting abuse, diagnosing errors,
					maintaining WebSocket connections, and protecting tokens. The legal basis is the
					legitimate interest in securing and maintaining the service.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Local app data:</strong> keeping your settings, token, profiles, and replay
					history on your device. The legal basis is the legitimate interest in providing the
					local features you enable.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Data requests:</strong> handling access, deletion, security, or incident
					requests. The legal basis is compliance with applicable legal obligations and the
					legitimate interest in documenting responses.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Website:</strong> serving public pages, protecting traffic, and remembering a
					theme preference strictly tied to the interface. The legal basis is legitimate interest
					and, for this personalization storage, the exemption for trackers that are strictly
					necessary or expected by the user.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Administrators who install MemeOver should clearly inform server members, for example in
				the server rules or watched channel descriptions, which channels are watched, which
				categories of content are transmitted, and whether commands such as
				<code>/memeover secret</code> are available. MemeOver does not replace Discord
				permissions: administrators remain responsible for choosing channels where members can
				reasonably expect their media, short text, and reactions to appear on connected overlays.
			</p>
			<p>
				If <code>/memeover setup</code> is used without specifying a channel, the bot may watch
				every server channel it can access. This global setup should only be used on servers
				where that information is explicit to members. Administrators should avoid configuring
				MemeOver in channels where sensitive, private, financial, health-related, or
				minor-related data may be posted without a reasonable expectation that it will appear on
				connected overlays.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Data Stored On Your Device</h2>
			<p>The desktop app stores data locally using Tauri's app storage:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>connection settings, including server ID, token, WebSocket URL, and display options;</li>
				<li>overlay profiles that you create;</li>
				<li>
					a local history of the last 50 displayed items, used for replay. This may include media
					URLs, Discord IDs, author display metadata, text captions, and timestamps;
				</li>
				<li>small interface preferences such as theme, language, onboarding, and dismissed notices.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				This local app data is not sent to MemeOver's website. You can clear media history from the
				History screen or the About screen. You can also remove the app's local data using your
				operating system's app-data controls.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Website Data</h2>
			<p>
				The website does not create accounts and does not run advertising or product analytics.
				It may store a theme preference in local storage so the selected theme persists. Cloudflare
				Workers and Cloudflare's network may process standard technical request data, such as IP
				addresses and routing metadata, to serve and protect the site.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Service Providers And Recipients</h2>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Discord:</strong> source of the server events processed by the bot.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Connected desktop clients:</strong> receive events for servers where they know the
					MemeOver server ID and token.
				</li>
				<li><strong>Better Stack:</strong> receives minimized operational logs for debugging and security.</li>
				<li><strong>Cloudflare:</strong> hosts and protects the public website on Workers.</li>
				<li><strong>GitHub:</strong> hosts the source code, releases, and update metadata.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Media providers:</strong> Discord CDN, Tenor, Giphy, or Imgur may receive requests
					when the desktop app loads media URLs.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Transfers Outside The European Union</h2>
			<p>
				The hosted bot is self-hosted in France. Even so, some providers or recipients may
				process data outside the European Union, including Cloudflare, Better Stack, GitHub,
				Discord, and media providers. MemeOver uses these services only for the purposes
				described in this policy: hosting and protecting the public website, minimized technical
				logs, release distribution, the Discord API, and media loading by desktop apps.
			</p>
			<p>
				For these services, MemeOver relies on the terms, data processing agreements, standard
				contractual clauses, adequacy decisions, or transfer safeguards published by the relevant
				providers when they are available and applicable. The publisher does not set up
				independent transfers outside those services.
			</p>
			<p>
				Connected desktop apps may also make direct requests to Discord CDN, Tenor, Giphy, or
				Imgur when they load received media URLs. Those requests are then handled by the relevant
				providers under their own terms and privacy policies.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Retention</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>Server configuration is kept until `/memeover remove` is used or deletion is requested.</li>
				<li>Realtime event payloads are not intentionally stored by the hosted bot after broadcast.</li>
				<li>Better Stack operational logs are kept for no more than 30 days.</li>
				<li>
					If a security incident requires evidence to be isolated, only the extracts strictly
					needed for analysis, remediation, and any required notifications may be kept in a
					separate incident file for up to 6 months after the incident is closed, then deleted
					or anonymized. A non-identifying summary may be retained to document corrective
					measures.
				</li>
				<li>Local media history is limited to 50 items and remains on your device until cleared.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The retention approach follows the data minimization and storage limitation principles
				described by the <a href="https://www.cnil.fr/fr/passer-laction/les-durees-de-conservation-des-donnees">CNIL</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Your GDPR Rights</h2>
			<p>
				You may request access to personal data about you, rectification, erasure, restriction of
				processing, or object to processing based on legitimate interest. Where the right to data
				portability applies, you may also request a structured copy of data you provided.
			</p>
			<p>
				To exercise these rights, contact the publisher with the information needed to identify
				the relevant configuration or logs, such as the Discord server ID. MemeOver has not
				appointed a data protection officer; the privacy contact point is the publisher listed
				above. Responses are provided within the time limits required by the GDPR.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you believe, after contacting us, that your rights are not respected, you may file a
				complaint with the <a href="https://www.cnil.fr/fr/plaintes">CNIL</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Your Choices And Deletion</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>Ask a server manager to run `/memeover remove` to delete the hosted bot's server configuration.</li>
				<li>Run `/memeover rotate` to invalidate an old connection token.</li>
				<li>Clear local history from the app when you no longer want replay data on your device.</li>
				<li>
					Request deletion of bot-side configuration or identifiable operational logs by contacting
					the publisher with the relevant Discord server ID. Do not send private media URLs in a
					public GitHub issue.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Security</h2>
			<p>
				MemeOver uses random connection tokens, WebSocket authentication, provider allowlists for
				media URLs, Discord CDN expiry checks, and minimized logging. You remain responsible for
				keeping your server token private and for configuring MemeOver on channels where members
				expect media to be shown on connected overlays.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Content Responsibility</h2>
			<p>
				MemeOver is a pass-through tool. People who send media or text through Discord remain
				responsible for the rights and legality of that content, including copyright, privacy,
				right of publicity, and server rules. MemeOver is not affiliated with Discord, Tenor,
				Giphy, Imgur, Better Stack, Cloudflare, or GitHub.
			</p>
		</section>
	</LegalDocument>
</BaseLayout>
